## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical ability to extend a docker-dca setup with a compliant, low-latency data-redaction path.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config-driven per-tenant policies
- Sidecar pattern and in-memory caching
- Streaming JSON redaction without full buffering
- Nginx-edge integration and TLS context

## Code Example
```go
package main
import (
  "encoding/json"
  "fmt"
)
func redact(m map[string]interface{}, fields map[string]bool){
  for k,v := range m {
    if fields[k] { m[k] = "***REDACTED***"; continue }
    if mm, ok := v.(map[string]interface{}); ok { redact(mm, fields) }
    if arr, ok := v.([]interface{}); ok {
      for _, it := range arr { if sub, ok := it.(map[string]interface{}); ok { redact(sub, fields) } }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test nested JSON redaction with large bodies?
- How would you monitor redaction performance without affecting latency?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to drive feature-flag configurations from Docker Configs in a multi-DC setup, plus per-tenant routing and safe fallbacks.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Configs for per-tenant flags
- X-Tenant header routing and fallback strategies
- In-process caching with TTL and config-driven feature gates

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js Express snippet to load tenant flags from docker config mounted at /config
app.use((req,res,next)=>{
  const t = req.header('X-Tenant');
  let flags = cache.get(t);
  if(!flags){
    try{
      const raw = fs.readFileSync(`/config/feature-flags-${t}.json`, 'utf8');
      flags = JSON.parse(raw);
      cache.set(t, flags);
    }catch(e){
      flags = globalDefaults;
    }
  }
  req.flags = flags;
  next();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you invalidate tenant flag cache when the Docker Config changes without restarting the API?
- How would you test this across two DCs with Traefik and simulate Config updates?

